		reflection_order_ir_convolution_mode = 0 ;\
\
		\cf5 //\cf2 \
		\cf5 // The impulse response assigned to each reflection order can be used as-is, \cf2 \
		\cf5 // i.e. unmodified, or can be convolved with other responses adjacent to it\cf2 \
		\cf5 // in the sequence of order-organized responses. A value of 0 for the parameter\cf2 \
		\cf5 // above outputs the original response. A value of -1, however causes the response\cf2 \
		\cf5 // to be convolved with the response of its immediately lesser order. Hence, \cf2 \
		\cf5 // with reflections limited to the 5 reflections (order 5), a mode setting of -1 would\cf2 \
		\cf5 // convolve order 5 with 4, 4 with 3, 3 with 2, and 2 with 1, with 1 output as-is. \cf2 \
		\cf5 // A setting of -2 would expand the convolution sequences, adding another lesser order,\cf2 \
		\cf5 // where available. (5 with 4 and 3, 4 with 3 and 2, etc.) Positive modes function \cf2 \
		\cf5 // similarly with the sequence of convolved responses progressing not to the lesser, \cf2 \
		\cf5 // but to the greater orders, when available. Hence, for a mode of 1, \cf2 \
		\cf5 // first-order reflections would be convolved with the second order response, i.e. \cf2 \
		\cf5 // 1 with 2, and 2 with 3. A mode of 2 would add greater orders to the sequence--1 with\cf2 \
		\cf5 // 2 and 3, etc. \cf2 \
		\cf5 //\cf2 \
		\cf5 // Convolution of an increasing number of adjacent orders will generally rarify the\cf2 \
		\cf5 // spectral characteristics of the reflection while lengthening its response and \cf2 \
		\cf5 // increasing the homogeneity of the resulting reflection order responses and \cf2 \
		\cf5 // subsequent reverberation. Convolution of classic room impulse responses, especially\cf2 \
		\cf5 // the same response applied to all orders, will produce a kind of increasingly \cf2 \
		\cf5 // metallic sound, which some may find interesting. \cf2 \
