\
		wall_ir_convolution_mode = -1 ;\
		\cf5 // (func)\cf2 \
		\cf5 // The convolution of wall impulse responses functions in a way similar to reflection \cf2 \
		\cf5 // order responses, with significant changes. Wall reflection patterns are \cf2 \
		\cf5 // distinguished not only by their reflection order, but by the sequence of walls \cf2 \
		\cf5 // off of which the source sound is reflected on its way to the speaker. This sequence of \cf2 \
		\cf5 // wall impulse responses determines how particular reflections will be represented,\cf2 \
		\cf5 // either through individual wall-identifying impulse responses or their reflection \cf2 \
		\cf5 // wall segment convolutions. \cf2 \
		\cf5 //\cf2 \
		\cf5 // Unlike reflection orders, use of a single wall impulse response is designated by a\cf2 \
		\cf5 // convolution mode number of either 1 or -1; for -1, the last wall of reflection \cf2 \
		\cf5 // reached before arrival at the speaker determines the impulse response used, for 1,\cf2 \
		\cf5 // the selection is reversed, with the first reflection wall being used. \cf2 \
		\cf5 //\cf2 \
		\cf5 // Convolution modes of greater magnitude, be they + or -, cause the respective last(-)\cf2 \
		\cf5 // or first(+) reflection wall to be convolved with its respective previous or following \cf2 \
		\cf5 // wall sequence impulse responses. For example, a source-to-speaker reflection wall sequence \cf2 \
		\cf5 // pattern of [2 3 1 7 5 2] and a convolution mode of -2 would result in the convolution\cf2 \
		\cf5 // and use of the impulse responses from walls 5 and 2, which occur at the end of the pattern.\cf2 \
		\cf5 // Conversely, a mode of 2 would use the convolution of 2 and 3, which are the first \cf2 \
		\cf5 // two walls encountered. As many walls are convolved together for use by the reflection\cf2 \
		\cf5 // as the mode and wall sequence pattern allow.  \cf2 \
		\cf5 //\cf2 \
		\cf5 // Convolution modes may exceed the reflection wall sequence length, which will not exceed\cf2 \
		\cf5 // the high order limit; in such cases, every wall reflection sequence will convolve\cf2 \
		\cf5 // all of its wall impulse responses together for use by the reflection in the output\cf2 \
		\cf5 // impulse response. For simplicity, the convolution mode of 0 designates the same thing\cf2 \
		\cf5 // for all high order limits. \cf2 \
\
		\cf5 // \cf2 \
